= Localhost dashboard
On the repo you'll find a folder: `dashboard`.
This folder contains a python script that emulates a SCADA dashboard.
You will use this dashboard all along the lab, the first step is to run it locally on your laptop and analyse the traffic with wireshark.
You must analyse the http traffic generated by this dashboard.

#task[Run and analyse dashboard locally][
  Write a small report with basic information as capture / display filter you used and what you found on packet.
  You can illustrate with wireshark screenshots and concise explanation. Once polling is started in the web page, your browser and the dashboard exchange http traffic on port #traffic-port-http. In this task, we focus on the content of the payload.

  #subtask[
    On PyCharm, open the `main.py` file in the `dashboard` folder and run it.
    Open http://localhost:8050 in your browser. In the "HTTP Traffic Generator" tab, click "Start HTTP Polling" to generate traffic.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Open Wireshark and select the loopback / localhost interface. Choose the right capture filter.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Select specific display filter to get only the `http` traffic and show in your report a packet in depth from the header to the json payload
  ]

][][
  + The server prints `Web Dashboard: http://localhost:8050`. Once polling runs, the browser sends `GET /api/lab/traffic?size=small&keep_alive=true` every second.

  + *Interface:* `Loopback: lo0` (macOS), `Adapter for loopback traffic capture` (Windows, Npcap), `lo` (Linux).

    *Capture filter* (BPF syntax): `tcp port 8050`.

  + *Display filter:* `http` works. Better, for only displaying the polling exchange:\
    `http.request.uri contains "/api/lab" || (http.response.code == 200 && json)`

    Captured request (frames 63 the reference capture):
    #shell[```
    Null/Loopback    Family: IP (2)
    Internet Protocol Version 4, Src: 127.0.0.1, Dst: 127.0.0.1
        Time to Live: 64    Protocol: TCP (6)    Flags: Don't fragment
    Transmission Control Protocol, Src Port: 58161, Dst Port: 8050, Len: 568
        Flags: 0x018 (PSH, ACK)
    Hypertext Transfer Protocol
        GET /api/lab/traffic?size=small&keep_alive=true HTTP/1.1
        Host: localhost:8050
        Connection: keep-alive
        User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; ...) Chrome/156.0.0.0 Safari/537.36
        Accept: */*
        Referer: http://localhost:8050/
    ```]

    Captured response pair (frames 65/67 of the reference capture):

    #shell[```
    Transmission Control Protocol, Src Port: 8050, Dst Port: 58161, Len: 151
    [2 Reassembled TCP Segments (353 bytes): #65(202), #67(151)]
    Hypertext Transfer Protocol
        HTTP/1.1 200 OK
        server: uvicorn
        content-type: application/json
        content-length: 151
        cache-control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
        connection: keep-alive
        [Request in frame: 63]    [Time since request: 1.251 ms]
    JavaScript Object Notation: application/json
        {"type": "server_telemetry",
         "timestamp": "2026-10-27T09:44:22.578626+00:00",
         "cpu_load_pct": 27.0, "memory_load_pct": 63.3,
         "requested_size": "small"}
    ```]

    The same request and response in Wireshark:
    #image("/res/img/01-homelab/solution/t3-http-request.png", width: 100%)
    #image("/res/img/01-homelab/solution/t3-http-response.png", width: 100%)

    Points worth mentioning and observations:
    - Source and destination IP are both `127.0.0.1`: the packets never leave the computer. The link layer is a pseudo "Null/Loopback" header, there is no Ethernet header and no MAC address.
    - The server port is 8050, the client port is an ephemeral port chosen by the OS (here 58161).
    - With `keep_alive=true` all polls reuse the same TCP connection (same client port, no new handshake per request).
    - The fields of the JSON payload are: `type`, `timestamp`, `cpu_load_pct`, `memory_load_pct` and `requested_size` (the `size` query parameter echoed back).
]

