][][
  + No deliverable. Wi-Fi must be off, otherwise the laptops may talk through the school network.

  + Any of these methods work depending on th OS:
    - Windows: `ipconfig` $->$ "Ethernet adapter … IPv4 Address".
    - macOS: `ipconfig getifaddr en<X>`, `ifconfig en<X>`, or System Settings $->$ Network $->$ Ethernet.
    - Linux: `ip a`.
    - Wireshark: display filter `dhcp` while replugging the cable; the DHCP ACK carries "Your (client) IP address".

    Expected is an address of the DHCP pool (e.g. `192.168.1.7`, mask `255.255.255.0`, gateway and DNS `192.168.1.1`).

  + Capture on the *Ethernet* interface (`en<X>` on macOS, "Ethernet" on Windows), filter `tcp port 8050` / display `http`. The HTTP request and JSON response are the same as on loopback. The differences:
    - The link layer is a real *Ethernet II* header with the MAC addresses of both laptops (instead of the "Null/Loopback" pseudo header).
    - The IP addresses are the LAN addresses of client and server, not `127.0.0.1`.
    - The `Host:` header contains the server IP instead of `localhost`.
    - Chrome might also drop some headers (e.g. `sec-ch-ua*` headers)
    - Both laptops see the same packets: one as sender, the other as receiver.

    Reference capture (laptop $->$ Raspberry Pi, same situation as laptop $->$ laptop):
    #shell[```text
    Ethernet II, Src: ASIXElectron_ba:9f:95 (c8:a3:62:ba:9f:95), Dst: RaspberryPiT_de:78:25 (d8:3a:dd:de:78:25)
    Internet Protocol Version 4, Src: 192.168.1.7, Dst: 192.168.1.4    Time to Live: 64
    Transmission Control Protocol, Src Port: 60684, Dst Port: 8050, Len: 360    (SYN: MSS 1460)
    Hypertext Transfer Protocol
        GET /api/lab/traffic?size=small&keep_alive=true HTTP/1.1
        Host: 192.168.1.4:8050
    ```]
]
#bonus[Lease time][
  Find your lease time. Show and explain in your report how you found this info. Explain what a lease is and the consequences of a shorter / longer lease.
  _Hint: look at `ipconfig /all` (Windows), `ipconfig getpacket <interface>` (macOS) or the DHCP option "IP Address Lease Time" in Wireshark._
][][
  Lease time on the edge router: *60 s* (`set service dhcp-server … lease 60`; the EdgeOS default would be 86400 s). It is kept short on purpose, so that the Raspberry Pi picks up its static mapping within a minute.

  - Windows: `ipconfig /all` $->$ "Lease Obtained" and "Lease Expires" (one minute apart).
  - macOS: `ipconfig getpacket en<X>` $->$ `lease_time (uint32): 0x3c` (= 60 s).
  - Wireshark: filter `dhcp`. With such a short lease no replug is needed: the client renews every ~30 s:
    #set text(size: 0.95em)
    #shell()[```text
     1   0.000  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.1  DHCP Request - Transaction ID 0x102dedab
     2   0.001  192.168.1.1 -> 192.168.1.7  DHCP ACK     - Transaction ID 0x102dedab
     3  29.005  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.1  DHCP Request - Transaction ID 0x102dedac
     4  29.006  192.168.1.1 -> 192.168.1.7  DHCP ACK     - Transaction ID 0x102dedac
    DHCP ACK:
        Option: (54) DHCP Server Identifier (192.168.1.1)
        Option: (51) IP Address Lease Time: 1 minute (60)
        Option: (1) Subnet Mask (255.255.255.0)
        Option: (3) Router: 192.168.1.1
        Option: (6) Domain Name Server: 192.168.1.1
    ```]

  A lease is a time-limited loan of an IP address by the DHCP server. The client tries to renew it with its server after half of the lease (T1), with any server after 87.5 % (T2), and must stop using the address when it expires.
  - *Shorter lease:* addresses of departed clients come back to the pool quickly (good for many short visitors, e.g. guest Wi-Fi), but more DHCP traffic and a client loses its address sooner if the server is down.
  - *Longer lease:* stable addresses and less traffic, but the pool can run out with stale leases of devices that are gone.
]

