#task[DNS for webpage on RPI][
  Since you now have a fixed IP address for the Raspberry Pi, you can access it with a name. We have already configured a DNS server on the router with a hostname for #raspberry-pi-ip. You can now access the dashboard on the Raspberry Pi with the name #raspberry-pi-dns-name. You will now analyse the traffic with Wireshark and write a small report about it.

  #subtask[
    Verify you have access to the dashboard as before but now with #raspberry-pi-dns-name.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Observe the DNS resolution and the traffic with Wireshark. Show and explain what you see in your own words in your report.
    #infobox[
      You can force nslookup to use a specific DNS server and bypass your cache: \
      e.g.: `nslookup dashboard.lan 192.168.1.1` uses the DNS server `192.168.1.1` to resolve `dashboard.lan`.
    ]
  ]
][][
  + `http://dashboard.lan:8050` shows the same dashboard as\  `http://192.168.1.4:8050`.

  + The router forwards all DNS queries of the laptop (Teams, Google, …), so `dns` alone is too noisy. Display filter:\
    `dns.qry.name contains "dashboard" || (ip.addr == 192.168.1.4 && http.request.uri == "/")`

    Reference capture (Chrome opens `http://dashboard.lan:8050`, then reloads twice):
    #text(size: 0.7em)[
      #shell[```text
      24  2.257  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.1  DNS  Standard query 0xe7bf HTTPS _8050._https.dashboard.lan
      25  2.257  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.1  DNS  Standard query 0x3387 A dashboard.lan
      27  2.258  192.168.1.1 -> 192.168.1.7  DNS  Standard query response 0x3387 A dashboard.lan A 192.168.1.4
      28  2.261  192.168.1.1 -> 192.168.1.7  DNS  Standard query response 0xe7bf No such name HTTPS ...
      32  2.262  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.4  HTTP GET / HTTP/1.1       Host: dashboard.lan:8050
      55  2.266  192.168.1.4 -> 192.168.1.7  HTTP HTTP/1.1 200 OK  (text/html)
     151  5.450  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.4  HTTP GET / HTTP/1.1       <- reload, no new DNS query
     213 10.474  192.168.1.7 -> 192.168.1.4  HTTP GET / HTTP/1.1       <- reload, no new DNS query

      Domain Name System (response)                         (frame 27)
          Transaction ID: 0x3387
          Flags: 0x8580 Standard query response, No error   <- authoritative answer
          Queries:  dashboard.lan: type A, class IN
          Answers:  dashboard.lan: type A, class IN, addr 192.168.1.4
                    Time to live: 0 (0 seconds)
      ```]
    ]

    The same capture in Wireshark:
    #image("/res/img/01-homelab/solution/t8-dns-response.png", width: 100%)
    - The browser cannot connect to a name: it first asks the DNS server received by DHCP (the router, `192.168.1.1`) for the address of `dashboard.lan`.
    - Query and response use UDP port 53 and share the same transaction ID. The response repeats the question and carries the answer record `A 192.168.1.4`.
    - The router answers itself (authoritative, from its own host table) instead of forwarding the query to the Internet, with a TTL of 0 s.
    - Afterwards the traffic is exactly the one of the previous task, sent to #raspberry-pi-ip. The name only appears in the `Host:` header of the HTTP request.
    - On reload no new DNS query appears although the TTL is 0: the browser / OS keeps the answer for a short time in its own cache. `nslookup dashboard.lan 192.168.1.1` always sends a real query (2 packets: `A dashboard.lan` $->$ `A 192.168.1.4`); flushing the cache has the same effect for the browser:
      - Windows: `ipconfig /flushdns`
      - macOS: #text(size: 0.95em)[`sudo dscacheutil -flushcache; sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder`]
]
